        "type": "text",
        "text": "A is fine. What might come later is actually the ability to connect to something like fluid voice or something that does speech to text. Or we could even build our own tool to do that, which could be pretty cool as well And therefore, allow exporting a transcription and a summary, but let's get to that later.."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "assistant",
    "timestamp": "2026-08-15T17:54:28.852Z",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Noted — and that fits the architecture perfectly: once sessions are stored as stems + metadata, a \"transcription\" step is just another *export target* (like M4A), whether that's a local Whisper model, an API service like Fluid Voice, or something we build later. I'll keep that seam open in the design but keep v1 focused.\n\n**Next question — how your friends will get the app.** This matters because it affects signing and packaging:\n\n**A)** **Direct download (DMG)** — you build it, notarize it, friends drag it into /Applications. Simplest, no App Store restrictions, standard for audio utilities (Audio Hijack, Loopback, etc. all distribute this way). Needs an Apple Developer ID certificate for notarization (~$99/yr) to avoid scary Gatekeeper warnings.\n\n**B)** **App Store** — automatic updates and discovery, but the sandbox has real friction with system audio capture (most pro audio apps avoid it for this reason), and review cycles slow you down.\n\n**C)** **Ad-hoc for now** — build and share unsigned/debug builds to close friends (right-click → Open to bypass Gatekeeper), worry about distribution later.\n\nMy lean: design for **A**, but start with **C** for the first usable version — friends right-click-open the early builds, and you add notarization once it's worth it. Are you OK with that, and do you already have a paid Apple Developer account?"
      }
    ],
    "model": "glm-5.3",
    "stopReason": "stop"
  }
]

[
  {
    "role": "user",
    "timestamp": "2026-08-15T17:56:15.670Z",
