    "role": "assistant",
    "timestamp": "2026-08-15T17:56:15.967Z",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Great — distribution settled: DMG + Developer ID notarization (note: that step needs the paid Apple Developer account when we get there; unsigned local builds until then).\n\nI now have what I need. Let me present the design in two parts, checking with you after each.\n\n---\n\n## Design — Part 1: Architecture & data flow\n\n**Working name:** \"Stems\" (easily changed later).\n\n**Platform floor:** macOS 14.4+ (the public Core Audio Process Tap API requires it — assume your friends are on 14.4 or newer; shout if not).\n\n**The recording model — the core idea of the whole app:**\n\n1. **Record time: capture everything selected, as independent stems.** Each source (Chrome, Zoom, Spotify, microphone…) gets its own capture chain and its own WAV file in a session folder, plus a `manifest.json` (source names, start timestamps, sample rates). All chains start on one trigger, so tracks align by timestamp. Taps are **non-destructive**: audio keeps playing to the speakers normally while recording — no BlackHole-style re-routing, no silence.\n\n2. **Export time: mix in the app, after the session.** The session view lists tracks with checkboxes; export produces a combined M4A/WAV mix of the ticked tracks, or individual files, or groups. Mixing happens from the stems on disk; nothing is decided at record time.\n\n**Per-app capture (the macOS-specific part):** each app source = a Core Audio *process tap* on that process + an aggregate device + an IOProc pulling PCM, written straight to disk via `ExtAudioFile`. Sources are enumerated from running apps (NSWorkspace) plus the default mic. A source that quits mid-session just ends its stem; the session continues.\n\n**Component layout (small, well-bounded modules):**\n\n- `RecorderEngine` — owns taps/capture chains; one API: \"start session with these sources\", \"stop\". This is the *only* file that talks Core Audio.\n- `SourceRegistry` — enumerates apps + input devices, tracks what's running.\n- `SessionStore` — session folders + manifest read/write.\n- `ExportEngine` — mixdown + M4A/WAV encoding (AVFoundation).\n- `MenuBarController` + SwiftUI `MainWindow` — the two faces of the UI (quick record with last-used sources / full control).\n\nThis boundary is deliberate: a v2 browser-extension tab source becomes *just another source type* feeding the engine, and a future transcription step is *just another export target* — no rewrites.\n\nDoes this architecture look right before I cover the UI and edge cases?"
      }
    ],
    "model": "glm-5.3",
    "stopReason": "stop"
  },
